# Icons and images

Use an icon or an image only when it helps someone identify, find or check something faster than words alone. Always put the information in text as well, so it reaches people who cannot see the image.

Most dashboards work best with few images. Write clear names and states first. Then add an image where it answers a real question, such as which light is on or who is at the front door.

## Choose the right kind of image

| Kind | Use it to | Owned by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Icon | Help people recognise a device, room or action they already know by name | This page |
| Photograph | Show what a real thing looks like, such as a device to find in a cupboard | This page |
| Camera image | Show what is happening at a place now, such as the front door | [Cameras, streams and snapshots](../home-assistant/cameras.html) |
| Floor plan | Show where rooms and devices are in the home | This page and [Pictures, rich content and embedded pages](../home-assistant/pictures-and-embedded-content.html) |
| Illustration | Explain something complex in a simple way, such as where to put a motion sensor | [Illustration](illustration.html) |
| Chart | Show how a reading changes over time | [Data visualisation](brand-data.html) |

## Icons

Use Home Assistant's own icons. They come from the Material Design Icons set, written with an `mdi:` prefix. Keep an icon's meaning the same on every dashboard.

Always show an icon with a visible name and state. A yellow bulb alone does not say which light it is, or whether it is on, off or unavailable.

This YAML gives a Tile card a clear name and a lamp icon. The card still shows the state, such as On or Unavailable, in words.

```yaml
type: tile
entity: light.hallway_lamp
name: Hallway lamp
icon: mdi:floor-lamp
```

### Choose an icon

| Do | Do not |
| --- | --- |
| Keep a device's native icon and the state icons that go with it, such as an open and a closed door | Replace every device icon with one shape to make the dashboard look neat |
| Use one icon for one meaning, such as `mdi:magnify` only for search | Use the same icon for 2 different actions |
| Add a visible label to an action people may not recognise | Rely on a tooltip to explain an icon-only button |
| Use one set of icons for a set of controls | Mix emoji, font symbols and hand-drawn icons in one row |
| Keep warning, error and manufacturer colours that carry meaning | Recolour every icon to the theme colour |

These pairs show 4 of these choices. The first shows the same light as an icon on its own and as an icon with its name and state, so you can see what the label adds.

<%= colour_example('images-icon-do-dont', callout: false) %>

Give an icon-only control an accessible name and a touch target of at least 44px. The icon can be smaller than the target, so a 24px icon can sit in a 44px button.

