## Photographs and camera images

Use a photograph when it matters what a real thing looks like. For example, show the boiler's reset button in a household guide, so someone can find it. Do not use stock photographs or a photograph that only sets a mood.

Show a camera image only where someone needs to check a place, such as a security view. Say which camera it is and when the picture was taken. A snapshot from 9 minutes ago is not live. [Cameras, streams and snapshots](../home-assistant/cameras.html) explains snapshot times, streams and privacy.

## Keep text out of images

Put words in text, not inside a picture. Text in an image cannot be resized, recoloured, copied, translated or read by every screen reader, and it goes blurry when someone zooms in.

Do not use a picture to show:

- a reading or state, such as “Front door: locked”
- the steps of an instruction
- a screenshot of an automation, when the steps could be written out

### When an image may contain text

Text in an image is fine when it is not the point of the image, such as a label on a boiler in a photograph. Write alt text that gives the meaning of the image, not the label.

Text is part of the image when removing it would change what the image means. For example:

- a household's own crest or logo, where the words are part of the design
- a photograph of the label on a device, so someone can match the model number

Even then, put any information people need, such as the model number, in text nearby.

### Other ways to show text with an image

- Put real text on top of the image with HTML, and check the contrast between the text and every part of the image under it, at every screen size.
- Draw the image as an SVG, so any text in it stays sharp when people zoom in. Check its contrast too.

## Floor plans, charts and diagrams

Give every floor plan, chart and diagram a short text description and, where it holds data, the same facts in text. Make that text visible to everyone, not only to screen readers.

The Picture card has an `alt_text` option. Use it for a photograph or a plan with a single meaning.

```yaml
type: picture
image: /local/garden-plan.png
alt_text: Back garden. The shed is at the far end, with the water butt on its left.
```

The Picture elements card has no alt text option. Put a heading or a Markdown card next to the floor plan that lists the rooms and the devices that matter, so people can use the dashboard without seeing the plan.

This example shows a floor plan with its sensors. Notice that each kind of sensor has its own shape, not only its own colour, and that the list under the plan gives the same facts in text.

<%= colour_example('images-floor-plan', callout: false) %>

[Data visualisation](brand-data.html) explains how to give a chart a text equivalent.

## Alt text

Give every image an `alt` attribute. Write alt text for an image that carries information. Leave it empty, `alt=""`, when the image adds nothing that the text around it does not already say.

| Image | Alt text |
| --- | --- |
| A floor plan or photograph that gives information | Describe what it shows that people need |
| A decorative picture | `alt=""` |
| An icon beside a visible label | `alt=""`, because the label already says it |
| An image inside a link, where the link text names the destination | `alt=""` |

This example shows an informative illustration. Notice that the alt text names the rooms and objects, and the caption says where they are and that it does not show a real device state.

<%= example('local-image-purpose') %>

The homepage uses the same artwork as decoration, with `alt=""`. Its source and rights are recorded in `docs/data/illustration-assets.json`.

[Images](../content/formatting-content/images.html) in the writing guide explains how to word alt text, with examples.

## Household identity images

A household or business name can be used on its own, with no image. If a household adds a crest or logo, keep its proportions and colours, and keep the name readable without it. [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html) explains how. The civica artwork belongs to [Project identity](brand.html).
